{# ── Chapter: Hook ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="hook">
  <h2>Deleting one feature meant editing files in four different folders</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A team is told to remove the "saved searches" feature, it's being replaced. Someone opens <code>components/</code> and deletes <code>SavedSearchList.jsx</code> and <code>SavedSearchCard.jsx</code>. Then <code>hooks/</code>, to delete <code>useSavedSearches.js</code>. Then <code>utils/</code>, to find <code>formatSavedSearchLabel.js</code> buried between forty other unrelated helper files. Then <code>services/</code>, for the API client. Four folders, four separate hunts, and no single place that told them "this is everything saved-searches touches."
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      Organizing by technical layer, <code>components/</code>, <code>hooks/</code>, <code>utils/</code>, <code>services/</code>, groups files by what kind of code they are, not by what they're for. Every feature ends up with its code smeared across all four folders, and every one of those folders ends up containing pieces of every feature, interleaved with no boundary between them.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The deeper cost isn't just navigation friction, it's that layer-based folders provide no structural signal about coupling. Nothing stops <code>utils/formatSavedSearchLabel.js</code> from being imported by an unrelated feature's component, because the folder structure never expressed that it belonged to saved-searches in the first place, it just looked like "a util," available to anyone.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Concept ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="concept">
  <h2>Group by what the code is for, not what kind of code it is</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A feature-sliced layout puts a feature's component, hook, util, and API client together, in one folder: <code>features/saved-searches/</code> holds <code>SavedSearchList.jsx</code>, <code>SavedSearchCard.jsx</code>, <code>useSavedSearches.js</code>, <code>formatSavedSearchLabel.js</code>, and its API client, all in one place. Deleting the feature means deleting one folder.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      The organizing question flips from "what type of file is this" to "what does this belong to." A shared <code>components/</code> or <code>utils/</code> folder still exists, but it's reserved for things genuinely used by more than one feature, a <code>Button</code>, a <code>formatDate</code> helper, not a dumping ground every feature's incidental helper ends up in by default.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      This is the same principle behind bounded contexts in domain-driven design applied to folder structure: a feature slice's internal files are an implementation detail of that feature, and a real feature-sliced convention (feature-sliced design, Nx's module boundaries, similar prior art) typically enforces that other slices can only import from a slice's public entry point, not reach into its internals directly, turning an informal convention into something a lint rule can actually check.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

{# ── Chapter: Story ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="story-1">
  <h2>The utils folder that had become an unofficial second app</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      A growing static site's <code>utils/</code> folder had 63 files in it after two years, one flat list, alphabetical, no subfolders. Nobody could tell from the folder listing which utils belonged to the blog, which belonged to the pricing calculator, and which were genuinely shared. New engineers routinely wrote a near-duplicate helper because they didn't realize an existing one already did almost the same thing, it was buried at position 40 of 63.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A migration moved each util into the feature folder of its actual single caller, keeping only genuinely multi-feature helpers in a slimmed-down shared <code>utils/</code>. The flat file count there dropped from 63 to 9, and the remaining 9 were things like <code>formatDate</code> and <code>debounce</code> that legitimately earned their shared status by being used in five or more features.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      The migration also surfaced coupling nobody had tracked: three "single-feature" utils turned out to have a second, unexpected caller in a completely unrelated feature, imported directly from the old flat <code>utils/</code> path because nothing prevented it. Moving those into their true owning feature's folder, then re-exporting from a proper shared location for the second caller, made that cross-feature dependency visible and intentional instead of an accident of folder structure.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

