{# ── Chapter: FE Depth ── #}
<section class="case-chapter" data-chapter="fe-depth">
  <h2>Where the real shared code should live, and where the boundary bites</h2>
  <div class="tone-junior">
    <p>
      Not everything belongs inside a feature folder, things truly used by every feature (a design-system Button, a date formatter, the app's root layout) still belong in a shared location. The test is simple: does more than one feature genuinely need this, or does it just feel more "official" sitting in a shared folder? If only one feature uses it today, it starts in that feature's folder, and moves to shared only when a second feature actually needs it.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-mid">
    <p>
      A feature-sliced structure earns its value fastest at the point of deletion or hand-off, "remove this feature" or "here's the folder, it's yours now" become one-line, low-risk operations instead of a multi-folder archaeology dig. It costs a little more thinking up front: a new file requires deciding "whose is this" instead of just "what type is this," which is a real, if small, tax on day-to-day authoring speed.
    </p>
  </div>
  <div class="tone-staff">
    <p>
      Enforce the boundary, don't just adopt the folder names, a feature-sliced layout with no import restriction degrades back into the same coupling problem over time, just with different folder names, once someone reaches directly into <code>features/saved-searches/formatSavedSearchLabel.js</code> from an unrelated feature instead of going through a shared location. An ESLint rule restricting cross-slice imports to each slice's declared public entry point (an <code>index.js</code> re-export, for example) turns the convention into something CI actually catches.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

